 desc='<b>Heideheim</b> is a small patch of heath in the wooded country between Norg and Peest, with a name that betrays its origin as part of a villa or summer house \u2014 in the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries city dwellers liked to buy a piece of Drenthe heath and build a summer retreat on it, and the romantic naming belongs to that fashion. What remains is an <b>open stretch of ling heath</b> with grass-encroached parts, fringed by Scots pine and self-sown birch. Such small heath relics are ecologically more valuable than their area suggests: they act as <b>warm, sunny islands</b> in an otherwise closed woodland landscape, on which <b>viviparous lizard, tiger beetle and heath butterflies</b> depend. Management consists of periodic <b>sod cutting and birch removal</b> \u2014 without it the heath would be woodland within two decades. For the walker it is a pleasant break in the forest, with a small but convincing purple carpet in August.',
