 tags=['Drenthe \u00b7 Aa en Hunze', 'Esdorp \u00b7 village green, Romanesque church and brook valley', 'list 33 \u00b7 no. 157'],
 loc='\U0001f4cd Anloo, between Gasteren and Annen \u00b7 Esdorp with surrounding landscape \u00b7 Large area',
 desc='<b>Anloo</b> counts as one of the most intact <b>esdorpen</b> in Drenthe and perhaps the most beautiful. The village groups around a <b>green with centuries-old pedunculate oaks</b> and the <b>Magnus church</b>, whose tuff nave dates from the <b>eleventh century</b> \u2014 making it one of the oldest churches in the northern Netherlands. Anloo was an administrative and religious centre early on: legal assemblies gathered here and the old route across the Hondsrug passed through. The surrounding landscape survives complete: the <b>domed arable field</b> south of the village, hedgebanks, the strubben and heaths on the ridge, and to the west the valley of the <b>Drentsche Aa</b> with its hay meadows. Dozens of <b>burial mounds</b> lie round about, and the famous <b>dolmen D9</b> near Annen is close by. Ecologically Anloo links the dry Hondsrug with the wet brook valley \u2014 a gradient exploited by badger, roe deer, yellowhammer and countless insects.',
